      content:
          '**Electrical Burn Pathophysiology**:\n\n1. **Electrical vs Thermal Burns**: Electrical burns cause deep tissue injury along the current pathway that is far more extensive than visible surface burns. The "iceberg" analogy — surface wounds underestimate internal damage. Current follows the path of least resistance: nerves > blood vessels > muscle > skin > fat > bone.\n\n2. **Tissue Damage Mechanisms**:\n- **Joule heating**: Current passing through tissue generates heat proportional to resistance. Nerves and vessels are preferentially damaged because current concentrates there.\n- **Electroporation**: Cell membrane disruption from electrical field — causes delayed cell death even in tissue that initially appears viable.\n- **Vascular damage**: Thrombosis of small and medium vessels causes delayed tissue necrosis. This is why compartment syndrome develops hours after injury.\n\n3. **Compartment Syndrome**: Massive muscle edema from rhabdomyolysis and direct electrical damage causes compartment pressures to rise. Bilateral forearm compartments are classic for hand-to-foot electrical pathway. Fasciotomy must occur within 6 hours to prevent irreversible ischemic damage.\n\n4. **Nerve Injury Prognosis**:\n- **Right median nerve (complete block at wrist)**: Entry wound was at the right palm — direct electrical and thermal injury to the nerve. Prognosis is guarded. If the nerve is in continuity, recovery may occur over 6-18 months (axonal regeneration at 1mm/day). EMG at 3-4 months looking for nascent MUPs is key. If no recovery by 6 months, surgical exploration with nerve grafting or tendon transfers should be considered.\n- **Left ulnar nerve (axonotmesis at elbow)**: Current pathway caused thermal injury. Axonotmesis (Sunderland Grade II-III) has better prognosis than neurotmesis. Expect some recovery over 6-12 months. Serial EMG every 3 months to monitor reinnervation.\n\n5. **Delayed Complications of Electrical Injury**:\n- Cataracts (can develop months to years later — needs ophthalmology screening)\n- Cardiac arrhythmias (rare after initial 48 hours but monitor)\n- Neuropsychological changes (attention, memory, processing speed)\n- Peripheral neuropathy (can be progressive)\n- Heterotopic ossification at joints near current pathway',
